    },
    {
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "seed": 231732163,
      "version": 119,
      "versionNonce": 1573488662,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786482328016,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "id": "t105",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 64,
      "y": 4044,
      "width": 1058.6953125,
      "height": 332.5,
      "text": "1) Make principal type first-class: HUMAN, PIPELINE, AI_AGENT. Humans authenticate through OIDC/PKCE with\nMFA or WebAuthn when risk requires it. Pipelines obtain federated workload identity and exchange it for short-\nlived credentials rather than storing static keys. Agents authenticate as workloads and receive attenuated\ndelegated tokens containing task and on-behalf-of context.\n\n2) At ~500K active tokens, keep the data plane local: verify signed short-lived tokens, audience, scope, sender\nbinding, cached security epoch, and emergency JTI denylist without a central database call on each request.\n\n3) Continuously stream auth telemetry to detect refresh reuse, sender-binding mismatch, human location/device\nanomalies, pipeline execution outside expected infrastructure, and agent tool/scope violations.\n\n4) When compromise occurs: contain first, revoke JTI/family, bump security epoch when necessary, globally\ninvalidate caches, alert the owner/security team, then require fresh identity proof. A compromised token is never\nenough to mint its own replacement.",
      "fontSize": 19,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Make principal type first-class: HUMAN, PIPELINE, AI_AGENT. Humans authenticate through OIDC/PKCE with MFA or WebAuthn when risk requires it. Pipelines obtain federated workload identity and exchange it for short-lived credentials rather than storing static keys. Agents authenticate as workloads and receive attenuated delegated tokens containing task and on-behalf-of context.\n\n2) At ~500K active tokens, keep the data plane local: verify signed short-lived tokens, audience, scope, sender binding, cached security epoch, and emergency JTI denylist without a central database call on each request.\n\n3) Continuously stream auth telemetry to detect refresh reuse, sender-binding mismatch, human location/device anomalies, pipeline execution outside expected infrastructure, and agent tool/scope violations.\n\n4) When compromise occurs: contain first, revoke JTI/family, bump security epoch when necessary, globally invalidate caches, alert the owner/security team, then require fresh identity proof. A compromised token is never enough to mint its own replacement.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "aE",
      "roundness": null
    },
